# Host profiles and operations configs are tracked. They were ignored as "one
# workstation's view of one Host", and two values were: absolute paths to this
# laptop's SSH key and known_hosts. Those are `~/.ssh/...` now, which every
# operator's own machine resolves, and nothing else in them was local — every
# other path is relative to the repository or to the operator's home.
#
# Ignoring them cost more than it protected. What a board is, what it can do,
# its unit topology and its settings overlays are reviewed product decisions,
# and they existed on exactly one machine: nobody else could reproduce the Host
# that was actually deployed, and no test could load the config that actually
# runs — `test_paths.py` loaded an example instead and said so in its docstring.
#
# The secrets never were here. They live under `.eidolon-ops/`, which stays
# ignored; the configs only name relative paths into it.
#
# One operator's record of their own runs, beside the profile it belongs to.
# Ignored because it is a log rather than a decision, plus one thing this
# repository learned the hard way: an untracked file is a dirty worktree, and a
# dirty worktree is something `require_clean` refuses to seal a release from.
config/hosts/runs/

# The console's interface is a build artifact; `cd web && npm run build` makes it.
src/eidolon_ops/console/static/
web/node_modules/

# Finder writes these anywhere a directory is opened, and an untracked file is
# a dirty worktree: `require_clean` would refuse to seal a release because
# somebody looked in a folder.
.DS_Store
